<!-- 

ANYTHING BETWEEN "<!-—" AND "—->"
WILL NOT BE LOADED BY THE GAME

you can use these 'comment' tags to remove tracks you
aren't using while still keeping the code handy

if you want to add audio for a track that is 'commented out'
below (e.g. Sacrifice Room) you can either delete the
"<!-—" and "—->" tags around it or move it outside of the tags

any tracks that aren't in this file or are inside comment tags
will automatically be replaced by default music, so if you
don't have any custom audio for a track, either delete that track
from this file, or put the tracks code inside comment tags

if you want a track to be silent rather than using default music
just use an audio file with 1 second of silence


Volume
if a track is a bit loud or quiet compared to the other songs
you can add the mul="1.0" tag and change the number
using a number higher than 1 is louder, lower is quieter
e.g. mul="1.5"  mul="0.7"


Intros
tracks that loop can have intros, when the song starts it will play
the intro once then it will play the 'loop' audio, once the 'loop'
audio finishes it will restart without playing the intro again

if a song you're using has an intro you can cut it into a separate
ogg file and include it using the intro="" tag like this
<track name="Track Type" intro="intro.ogg" path="loop.ogg" loop="true" />


Layers
tracks can have layers, in most cases the layer will just fade in
over the song when there are enemies in the room, but some specific
tracks have special cases for layers (such as main menu and beast fight)

generally layers are added like this, with layer="" and layerintro="" tags
<track name="Track Type" intro="intro.ogg" path="music.ogg" layerintro="layerintro.ogg" layer="layer.ogg" loop="true" />
if your track doesn't use an intro you can remove the intro="" and layerintro="" tags

if you want the layer to play by itself when its triggered, rather than
playing on top of the base track you can add layermode="2" to the tracks code

also none of the tracks actually need layers, so for example
if you wanted the Beast fight to just be one song you can replace
<track name="Template Soundtrack Boss (Beast)" path="The_End_FAMINE.ogg" loop="true" layermode="2" layerfadespeed="0.01" mul="1.5">
    <layer path="The_End_PESTILENCE.ogg" mul="1.5" />
    <layer path="The_End_WAR.ogg" mul="1.5" />
    <layer path="The_End_DEATH.ogg" mul="1.5" />
    <layer path="The_End_BEAST.ogg" mul="2" />
</track>
with
<track name="Template Soundtrack Boss (Beast)" path="The_End_FAMINE.ogg" loop="true" />


Variants
if you want a certain track type to have multiple possible songs
you can duplicate the code for that track type, give it a different
audio file and add numbers to the end of the tracks names, e.g.
<track name="Template Soundtrack Basement 1" path="Basement1.ogg" loop="true" />
<track name="Template Soundtrack Basement 2" path="Basement2.ogg" loop="true" />
<track name="Template Soundtrack Basement 3" path="Basement3.ogg" loop="true" />
<track name="Template Soundtrack Basement 4" path="Basement4.ogg" loop="true" />

-->

